WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.506 --> 00:00:02.554
- Today, we're gonna talk
about time management hacks

00:00:02.554 --> 00:00:04.049
in law school.

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:05.854
There's absolutely no
substitute for hard work

00:00:05.854 --> 00:00:08.312
in law school, but I can
recommend a few things

00:00:08.312 --> 00:00:10.353
that will save you a hell of a lot of time

00:00:10.353 --> 00:00:12.871
to work smarter instead of harder.

00:00:12.871 --> 00:00:14.038
So stay tuned.

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:22.232
So let's talk about time
management hacks for law school,

00:00:22.232 --> 00:00:24.422
things that will help
you save a huge amount

00:00:24.422 --> 00:00:26.712
of time so that you can do the
things that will really help

00:00:26.712 --> 00:00:29.800
on the final exam and
get you better grades

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.598
at the end of the semester.

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:34.886
So we want to cut out all
the busy work possible.

00:00:34.886 --> 00:00:37.463
The first time saving tip
that I would recommend

00:00:37.463 --> 00:00:39.002
is to get prior outlines.

00:00:39.002 --> 00:00:41.745
Get old outlines that
are specifically tailored

00:00:41.745 --> 00:00:43.681
for your professor.

00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:45.464
You can talk to upperclassmen

00:00:45.464 --> 00:00:48.026
who have had your professor before.

00:00:48.026 --> 00:00:50.505
Or you can go to OutlineDepot.com.

00:00:50.505 --> 00:00:52.336
OutlineDepot.com is great.

00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:54.296
I have no affiliation with them,

00:00:54.296 --> 00:00:57.912
but it's a great website where
if you upload one outline,

00:00:57.912 --> 00:00:59.705
you're allowed to get a credit to download

00:00:59.705 --> 00:01:01.050
someone else's outline.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.136
And because so many people
have uploaded outlines

00:01:04.136 --> 00:01:08.158
to their database, odds
are they're going to have

00:01:08.158 --> 00:01:11.051
a prior outline for
your specific professor

00:01:11.051 --> 00:01:13.371
in the class that you're taking.

00:01:13.371 --> 00:01:15.076
So I would recommend getting
a handful of outlines

00:01:15.076 --> 00:01:17.756
for your specific professor
so that you can use those

00:01:17.756 --> 00:01:21.165
as a template to make your own outlines.

00:01:21.165 --> 00:01:23.884
It's no substitute for putting in the time

00:01:23.884 --> 00:01:26.780
learning the law, but it can
provide a great foundation

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.132
that you can add or subtract to.

00:01:29.132 --> 00:01:32.213
It's the beginning of the
process that you can use

00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:35.517
to save a lot of time instead
of doing it from scratch.

00:01:35.517 --> 00:01:39.177
By the same token, use commercial
outlines and case briefs.

00:01:39.177 --> 00:01:42.172
Things like Emanuel's for contracts.

00:01:42.172 --> 00:01:44.877
There are commercial outlines
in just about any class

00:01:44.877 --> 00:01:46.405
that you can think of.

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:48.028
They will save you a huge amount of time

00:01:48.028 --> 00:01:50.685
in learning the substantive law.

00:01:50.685 --> 00:01:54.718
And recently there have been
a preponderance of websites

00:01:54.718 --> 00:01:57.360
and databases out there
that will case brief

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.273
every single case you could
possibly come across for you.

00:02:01.273 --> 00:02:02.673
It can save you a huge amount of time.

00:02:02.673 --> 00:02:04.689
We don't advocate doing any case briefs.

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:06.377
We think they're a waste of time.

00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:09.230
But it's great to look
at other case briefs

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.799
to provide a foundation
before you read the case.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:16.031
It's great to understand
what the general idea

00:02:16.031 --> 00:02:19.224
of the case is before you
dig into your case book

00:02:19.224 --> 00:02:20.608
and read the case yourself.

00:02:20.608 --> 00:02:22.992
Tip number three: use Westlaw.

00:02:22.992 --> 00:02:24.447
Westlaw is great.

00:02:24.447 --> 00:02:26.815
You will use it all the time in practice,

00:02:26.815 --> 00:02:29.137
either Westlaw or LexisNexis.

00:02:29.137 --> 00:02:32.919
So you might as well get
familiar with it in law school.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.768
And one of the great
things that you can do is

00:02:34.768 --> 00:02:37.840
if you don't want to go the
commercial case brief route,

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.906
things like Quimbee for example,

00:02:39.906 --> 00:02:42.910
you could use Westlaw to look up the case

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.767
that your professor wants you to read.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:48.312
Even if the case is
old, Westlaw has an army

00:02:48.312 --> 00:02:51.326
of reference attorneys that
summarize every single case.

00:02:51.326 --> 00:02:54.621
You could just read
through the West head notes

00:02:54.621 --> 00:02:57.277
and get a good idea of
what the case is about.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:03:00.166
And it probably summarizes
the exact rule of law

00:03:00.166 --> 00:03:03.397
that you're gonna need to
learn for the final exam.

00:03:03.397 --> 00:03:05.583
So don't be afraid to use Westlaw.

00:03:05.583 --> 00:03:06.846
It's not cheating.

00:03:06.846 --> 00:03:09.062
It's a great way to practice something

00:03:09.062 --> 00:03:11.318
that you're gonna use all
the time in researching

00:03:11.318 --> 00:03:12.877
in the real world.

00:03:12.877 --> 00:03:15.942
Tip number four: outline from day one.

00:03:15.942 --> 00:03:17.135
This is controversial.

00:03:17.135 --> 00:03:20.112
A lot of people wait until
the very last few weeks

00:03:20.112 --> 00:03:21.447
of the semester before they outline.

00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:23.200
I thing that's a horrible mistake.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.278
If you outline every single weekend,

00:03:25.278 --> 00:03:27.574
you synthesize all of the substantive law,

00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:31.294
your notes with your commercial outlines,

00:03:31.294 --> 00:03:34.327
and you put it into a
form that you understand.

00:03:34.327 --> 00:03:36.460
You get that work done ahead of time,

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.384
it gives you so much more time
at the end of the semester

00:03:39.384 --> 00:03:41.134
to do practice exams,

00:03:42.587 --> 00:03:44.352
to memorize the black letter law

00:03:44.352 --> 00:03:46.988
that you're going to
use on the final exams.

00:03:46.988 --> 00:03:48.254
It's a huge advantage.

00:03:48.254 --> 00:03:49.686
It saves you a huge amount of time

00:03:49.686 --> 00:03:53.069
and creates more study time
at the end of the semester.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:57.714
Tip number five: take a
course on law school essays.

00:03:57.714 --> 00:04:00.669
There are clear strategies
for issue spotting exams

00:04:00.669 --> 00:04:03.941
that you can use on any
class whether it's an exam

00:04:03.941 --> 00:04:08.275
for contracts, or con law,
or torts, or whatever.

00:04:08.275 --> 00:04:12.047
Standard strategies will work
on any issue spotting exam.

00:04:12.047 --> 00:04:14.086
Legal Eagle has a course and
there are others out there

00:04:14.086 --> 00:04:15.807
that are also very good.

00:04:15.807 --> 00:04:19.006
It saves you a huge amount
of time because you can learn

00:04:19.006 --> 00:04:21.872
to take law school essays yourself.

00:04:21.872 --> 00:04:24.597
But you have to take a
number of practice exams.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:28.442
And often, professors only give
you so many practice exams.

00:04:28.442 --> 00:04:30.290
So it can save you a huge amount of time

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.554
to learn the strategies
up front so you don't have

00:04:33.554 --> 00:04:35.513
to brute force your way
through a huge number

00:04:35.513 --> 00:04:36.793
of practice exams.

00:04:36.793 --> 00:04:38.793
Because they take hours
and hours to get through

00:04:38.793 --> 00:04:41.009
every single practice exam.

00:04:41.009 --> 00:04:44.274
If you learn the strategies
and start taking practice exams

00:04:44.274 --> 00:04:46.135
at least halfway through the semester

00:04:46.135 --> 00:04:49.666
long before everyone else
starts taking practice exams,

00:04:49.666 --> 00:04:52.090
you will be miles ahead by the time

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.841
the study period rolls around.

00:04:53.841 --> 00:04:56.302
And you will save a huge amount of time

00:04:56.302 --> 00:04:59.609
before you have to take
your first final exam.

00:04:59.609 --> 00:05:01.921
So remember, you can learn the strategies

00:05:01.921 --> 00:05:04.202
that go into an issue
spotting exam the same way

00:05:04.202 --> 00:05:07.281
that you learn the strategies
for reading comprehension

00:05:07.281 --> 00:05:09.688
and logic games in LSAT.

00:05:09.688 --> 00:05:11.065
And if you learn those strategies

00:05:11.065 --> 00:05:13.856
and you start taking
practice exams sooner,

00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:15.603
it will save you a huge amount of time

00:05:15.603 --> 00:05:17.753
and you will be ready for your final exams

00:05:17.753 --> 00:05:20.154
much earlier than everybody else.

00:05:20.154 --> 00:05:22.937
And when you're graded on the
curve, that's what matters:

00:05:22.937 --> 00:05:25.225
being better than everybody else.

00:05:25.225 --> 00:05:26.952
So if you like this
video, please click like

00:05:26.952 --> 00:05:29.897
and subscribe for more
law school crushing tips.

00:05:29.897 --> 00:05:32.096
And also please download our checklist

00:05:32.096 --> 00:05:35.672
that will help you hack
your way through law school

00:05:35.672 --> 00:05:37.651
and save a huge amount of time.

00:05:37.651 --> 00:05:41.818
So thanks for stopping by from
everyone here at Legal Eagle.

